---
import Shell from '../../layouts/Shell.astro';
import { link } from '../../lib/links';
import { source } from '../../lib/source';
const rows = [
  {
    id: 'ornith-w2',
    model: 'Ornith 1.5 35B',
    quant: 'Q4_K_M',
    workload: 'W2 · Summarization',
    tokens: '256 / 128',
    normal: '50.27',
    fixed: 'Lookup · 82.19',
    adaptive: '80.08–80.84',
    note: 'Favorable input/output overlap. Fixed lookup is faster in the reported results.',
    section: '§8.3 A',
  },
  {
    id: 'ornith-w7',
    model: 'Ornith 1.5 35B',
    quant: 'Q4_K_M',
    workload: 'W7 · Creative prose',
    tokens: '256 / 128',
    normal: '49.75',
    fixed: 'Lookup · 48.84',
    adaptive: '49.10–49.18',
    note: 'Adaptive remains below normal. Fallback does not guarantee zero regression.',
    section: '§8.3 A',
  },
  {
    id: 'qwen-w3',
    model: 'Qwen3.8-Flash-Next',
    quant: 'UD-IQ4_XS',
    workload: 'W3 · JSON schema',
    tokens: '256 / 64',
    normal: '18.33',
    fixed: 'MTP · 23.43',
    adaptive: '23.52',
    note: 'Development result; the small difference from fixed MTP is not a demonstrated statistically significant win.',
    section: '§8.3 B',
  },
  {
    id: 'qwen-w8',
    model: 'Qwen3.8-Flash-Next',
    quant: 'UD-IQ4_XS',
    workload: 'W8 · Mathematical reasoning',
    tokens: '256 / 64',
    normal: '18.40',
    fixed: 'MTP · 23.94',
    adaptive: '23.68',
    note: 'Phase 6.6 adaptive was 24.31 tok/s. This Phase 6.6.1 result regresses versus that revision and fixed MTP.',
    section: '§8.3 B',
  },
];
---

<Shell
  property="guild"
  path="performance/"
  title="Guild performance — The measurement and its limits"
  description="Selected Phase 6.6.1 measurements on dual Xeon Gold 6130 and GTX 1070, with workload, quantization, baseline, and explicit limitations."
>
  <section class="subhero wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow">Evidence / performance</div><h1>
      The measurement.<br /><em>And its limits.</em>
    </h1><p class="lead">
      Selected reported results, not a universal speed claim. Every row below belongs to a specific
      model, workload, and decoding configuration.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="section wrap">
    <div class="grid-two">
      <div class="paper">
        <span class="section-label">Measured machine</span><h2
          style="font-size:2.5rem;margin-top:20px"
        >
          A large-RAM server.<br />An 8 GiB GPU.
        </h2><ul class="check-list">
          <li>CPU<span>2× Xeon Gold 6130 · 32C / 64T</span></li><li>
            Memory<span>376 GiB DDR4 · dual NUMA</span>
          </li><li>GPU<span>GeForce GTX 1070 · 8 GiB</span></li><li>
            Platform<span>Linux · AVX-512 capable</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div><div>
        <h2 style="font-size:2.8rem">Reading these results</h2><p style="margin-top:20px">
          Source: Phase 6.6.1 tables in the report snapshot at <code>140f6f7</code>. Engine change: <code
            >c7c8c79</code
          >; earlier Phase 6.6: <code>ed7a5db</code>. GEXEC active, CPU expert path, max draft 4,
          greedy generation. Qwen cross-window parity runs use <code>GUILD_IQ_MT_MIN=1</code>.
        </p><p style="margin-top:18px">
          These are decode tokens per second, not end-to-end request throughput. Ranges combine
          reported development runs and revisions; they are not confidence intervals or paired
          median/dispersion estimates.
        </p><p style="margin-top:18px">
          GEXEC artifacts were regenerated during tuning. Exact per-row calibration identity is not
          established here, limiting controlled cross-revision comparisons.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section wrap">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="section-label">01 / Selected development results</span><h2>
        The workload changes<br />the outcome.
      </h2>
    </div><div
      class="table-scroll"
      tabindex="0"
      role="region"
      aria-label="Benchmark results; scroll horizontally on small screens"
    >
      <table>
        <caption style="text-align:left;padding:15px;font-size:12px"
          >Phase 6.6.1 · decode tok/s · prompt / generated token counts</caption
        ><thead
          ><tr
            ><th scope="col">Model / quantization</th><th scope="col">Workload</th><th scope="col"
              >Prompt / gen</th
            ><th scope="col">Normal</th><th scope="col">Fixed strategy</th><th scope="col"
              >Adaptive</th
            ><th scope="col">Source</th></tr
          ></thead
        ><tbody
          >{
            rows.map((r) => (
              <tr>
                <>
                  <td>
                    {r.model}
                    <br />
                    <code>{r.quant}</code>
                  </td>
                  <td>{r.workload}</td>
                  <td>{r.tokens}</td>
                  <td>{r.normal}</td>
                  <td>{r.fixed}</td>
                  <td>{r.adaptive}</td>
                  <td>
                    <a
                      href={source(
                        'docs/performance/PHASE66_ADAPTIVE_SPECULATIVE_DECODING.md',
                        '#83-controlled-phase-66-vs-phase-661-benchmark-comparison',
                      )}
                    >
                      {r.section} ↗
                    </a>
                  </td>
                </>
              </tr>
            ))
          }</tbody
        >
      </table>
    </div><div class="grid-two" style="margin-top:25px">
      {
        rows.map((r) => (
          <div class="paper">
            <>
              <h3>
                {r.model} / {r.workload}
              </h3>
              <p>{r.note}</p>
            </>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div><div class="notice">
      <strong>Held-out counterexample:</strong> Ornith H4 (Riemann tensor) reports 50.16 tok/s normal,
      42.07 fixed lookup, and 47.92 adaptive. Adaptive is below normal by about 4.5%. The summary table
      does not provide complete per-run configuration; treat it as a reported observation rather than
      a reproducible paired estimate.
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section wrap">
    <div class="statement">
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow">02 / Boundaries of the evidence</span><h2>
          What these numbers<br />do not establish.
        </h2>
      </div><div>
        <p>
          80+ tok/s Ornith results rely on favorable speculative workloads with substantial reusable
          text. They are not general chat throughput, all-model performance, or measurements on a
          small-RAM consumer desktop.
        </p><p>
          Offline calibration measures CPU dispatch and lookup/retrieval microbenchmarks. Full
          verification curves, GPU/context costs, and concurrent-session scaling still include
          estimates. A doubled CPU job count is not a concurrent-session benchmark.
        </p><p>
          The website does not assert successful Phase 6.6.1 sanitizer, complete CUDA CTest,
          seeded-sampling, or concurrency validation without retained evidence. The engine report is
          available as a historical source, with those limits noted.
        </p><p>Ryzen 5 5600X / RTX 5070 results remain pending actual hardware measurement.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section wrap">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="section-label">03 / Source archive</span><h2>Read the engineering.</h2>
    </div><div class="pillars">
      {
        [
          ['phase6', 'Phase 6', 'Populated context, KV placement, and reference comparisons.'],
          ['phase65', 'Phase 6.5', 'CPU expert kernels, prefill, and end-to-end optimization.'],
          [
            'phase66',
            'Phase 6.6 / 6.6.1',
            'Adaptive decoding, calibration, reported workloads, and held-out cases.',
          ],
        ].map(([slug, title, text]) => (
          <article class="pillar">
            <>
              <h3>{title}</h3>
              <p>{text}</p>
              <a class="text-link" href={link('docs', `${slug}/`)}>
                Read complete source ↗
              </a>
            </>
          </article>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>
</Shell>
